number|title|condition|evidence|remediation|limitation|attack|nist|nist_rationale|sources|severity
001|An AD group editor can change access to a sensitive cloud application|An unapproved principal can write membership of an AD group AND its verified synchronized cloud group has an effective sensitive application or resource grant.|Source membership ACL; exact synchronized-group mapping; supported membership semantics; resource assignment and scope.|Restrict source-group membership management or use a separately governed cloud group for sensitive access.|Do not invent an Entra role-assignable synced group. This rule concerns supported application and resource grants.|T1098.007;T1078.004|AC-3;AC-6|Apply least privilege to the actual source of cloud group authorization.||High
002|Nested source-group administration expands a cloud resource grant|An unapproved editor controls an upstream group in a membership chain AND the target service actually honors that chain for the synchronized resource grant.|Complete membership chain; group types/scopes; synchronization output; documented target-service nesting semantics; effective grant.|Flatten or separately govern the sensitive authorization chain and remove unintended editors.|Not every cloud service honors nested membership. Unsupported nesting is not a traversable edge.|T1098.007|AC-3;AC-6|Review indirect authorization paths rather than only the group's direct owners.||High
003|An editable source attribute controls dynamic cloud group membership|An unapproved principal can write a synchronized attribute used by an active dynamic-group rule AND a supported value change would add an identity to a sensitive resource grant.|Attribute-specific effective write; synchronization mapping; exact dynamic rule; supported evaluation semantics; resource grant.|Restrict attribute authority and avoid low-assurance attributes for sensitive access decisions.|Do not execute rule text. A matching display name, unsupported attribute, or delayed/unconfirmed membership evaluation is not proof of access.|T1098;T1078.004|AC-3;CM-5;IA-4|Protect attributes used as authorization inputs and maintain trustworthy identifiers.||High
004|An AD-controlled owner can change a sensitive cloud group|A verified AD-control path reaches an Entra group owner AND that owner can effectively modify the group carrying a sensitive resource grant.|Source account control; cloud owner ID; group type; owner capabilities; active resource grants.|Use protected owners for sensitive groups and remove the source-account control path.|Ownership does not override every group restriction. Role-assignable and externally managed groups require separate permission checks.|T1098;T1098.007|AC-3;AC-6|Protect group-management authority and downstream resource access.||High
005|A cloud group editor can change privileged on-premises access|A cloud-managed group is provisioned to AD through a supported flow AND its resulting membership contributes effective administration of a sensitive on-premises asset AND an unapproved cloud principal can edit it.|Cloud membership authority; provisioning job and scope; exact AD target group; group nesting; effective host authorization.|Restrict cloud membership management and remove privileged AD nesting from broad provisioning scopes.|Do not assume retired Connect Group Writeback v2 or every cloud group is supported. Confirm the actual Cloud Sync path.|T1098.007;T1078.002|AC-3;AC-4;AC-6|Control authorization changes flowing from the cloud back into AD.||High
006|An editable synced group is an effective cloud-policy exclusion|An unapproved source-group editor can add a relevant identity to a group excluded from an enforced authentication policy AND no other effective policy restores the required protection.|Membership-control path; synchronized group ID; CA exclusion; user/resource context; combined enabled-policy evaluation.|Protect exclusion groups and minimize narrowly justified exceptions.|A single exclusion is not a bypass when another enabled policy enforces the requirement. Report-only policies do not restore protection.|T1098.007;T1078.004|AC-3;IA-2;CM-5|Protect exceptions that alter enforcement of authentication requirements.|MS-CA;SCUBA-AAD|High
007|On-premises attribute control weakens cloud device-based access|An unapproved principal controls a supported device or identity attribute used by an effective cloud access filter AND the change would admit a lower-assurance device to a sensitive resource.|Specific attribute ACL; source authority; supported sync flow; filter semantics; device state and policy context.|Use trustworthy device signals and protect the authority that supplies access-filter attributes.|Hybrid join is not device compliance. An editable AD computer object does not automatically confer a trusted cloud device claim.|T1098.005;T1078.004|IA-3;AC-3;CM-5|Protect device identity and the authorization signals derived from it.|MS-CA|High
008|A changed provisioning boundary leaves a sensitive group grant behind|An approved source-group removal or scope change should revoke access AND a current target membership or grant remains beyond the agreed propagation window.|Source change record; supported deletion/deprovision behavior; provisioning health; current target membership; sensitive grants.|Reconcile orphaned grants and repair the failed provisioning or deprovisioning workflow.|Scope exclusion does not always delete a target object. Evaluate configured semantics and intended access, not an assumed deletion rule.|T1078.004|AC-2;AC-3;CM-3|Keep authorization aligned with approved source and provisioning changes.||High
